    "summary": "High‑energy Europe ’72 with stellar Dark Star > Sugar Magnolia and tight jams",
    "blurb": "The Copenhagen show delivers a blazing China Cat Sunflower → I Know You Rider segue, a powerful Dark Star → Sugar Magnolia → Caution sequence, and the first ever performance of He's Gone. Jerry’s guitar soars, Phil drives with thunderous bass, and Keith’s piano adds majestic texture. All captured on crystal‑clear soundboard mixes that showcase the band at peak 1972 form.",
    "review": "April 17, 1972 at Tivoli Gardens finds the Dead in full flight during their Europe ’72 run. From the opening “Cold Rain And Snow” through a rousing first set, the band builds momentum that explodes on the classic China Cat Sunflower → I Know You Rider transition – Jerry’s lead lines sparkling over Keith’s bright piano‑organ blend. The second set packs a punch with “Casey Jones,” “Playing In The Band,” and a heartfelt rendition of “He’s Gone” (the song’s debut performance), while “Big Railroad Blues” roars with Bill’s propulsive drumming.\n\nThe third‑set centerpiece is a 30‑minute Dark Star that morphs seamlessly into Sugar Magnolia and then Caution, showcasing an inspired interplay between Garcia’s expressive Strat work and Keith’s jazzy chords. The set closes on a rollicking “Johnny B. Goode,” leaving the audience buzzing. Across all six soundboard sources the recording quality is superb – crisp stereo separation, balanced organ and guitar tones, and only negligible static in the premier Sirmick capture, making this one of the most accessible Europe ’72 documents.",
